From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 02:30:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: flowtable: fix bridging misclassification and make
 forward-path resolution non-fatal

Two issues in the Airoha flowtable offload path (introduced by the
bridging-support patch) prevent every flow from being added to the
flowtable, which in turn leaves the PPE/NPU with zero bound flows:

1. nft_flow_offload_is_bridging() classifies *any* frame whose
   destination MAC is present in the bridge FDB as bridging traffic.
   The bridge FDB always contains the bridge's own MAC, so every
   L3-routed frame whose next hop is the router (dst MAC == port MAC or
   == bridge master MAC) is misclassified as bridging and sent down the
   strict bridging offload path. That path resolves routes per direction
   and can fail; a failed direction drops the whole flow.

2. nft_dev_forward_path() returns -ENOENT when it cannot resolve the
   device forward path (e.g. no valid neighbour for the router's own
   address, or the resolved device is not listed in the flowtable).
   nft_flow_route() propagates that error, so the flow is never added to
   the flowtable. Mainline behaviour treats a failed forward-path lookup
   as non-fatal and keeps the default route, letting the flow be
   offloaded anyway.

Restore the mainline semantics:
- exclude frames addressed to the router itself from the bridging check,
  so routed traffic takes the ordinary routing path;
- make nft_dev_forward_path() non-fatal (return 0) when the device path
  cannot be resolved.

This lets established flows enter the flowtable and reach the Airoha PPE
driver so L2/L3 forwarding can be bound to the NPU.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
 net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_path.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_path.c b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_path.c
@@ -87,6 +87,20 @@
 	rcu_read_lock();
 	port = br_port_get_rcu(skb->dev);
 	if (port) {
+		/* Frames addressed to the router itself (its port MAC or the
+		 * bridge master MAC) are L3-routed, not bridged. The bridge
+		 * FDB always contains the local MACs, so without this check
+		 * every routed frame would be misclassified as bridging and
+		 * pushed through the strict bridging offload path, which can
+		 * drop the whole flow when the forward path cannot be
+		 * resolved.
+		 */
+		if (ether_addr_equal(dmac, skb->dev->dev_addr) ||
+		    ether_addr_equal(dmac, port->br->dev->dev_addr)) {
+			rcu_read_unlock();
+			return false;
+		}
+
 		vlan_id = nft_flow_offload_get_vlan_id(port, skb);
 		if (br_fdb_find_rcu(port->br, dmac, vlan_id))
 			bridging = true;
@@ -332,7 +346,7 @@
 	}
 
 	if (!info.indev || !nft_flowtable_find_dev(info.indev, ft))
-		return -ENOENT;
+		return 0;
 
 	route->tuple[!dir].in.ifindex = info.indev->ifindex;
 	for (i = 0; i < info.num_encaps; i++) {
